/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Equilibrium torsion is torque the member must carry to keep the structure standing.
   - Compatibility torsion comes only because members are cast together.
   - The exam test: release the twist in your mind.
   - In an uncracked solid rectangle the torsional shear stress is zero at the centre and largest at the middle of the long faces.
   - After cracking, the inside of the section does little.
   - A beam under bending and torsion can also fail by skew bending: a spiral crack on three faces and crushing along the fourth.
   - Ve is the equivalent shear (N); Vu the factored shear (N); Tu the factored torque (N·mm); b the breadth (mm); τve the equivalent shear stress (N/mm²); d the effective depth (mm).
   - If τve exceeds τc,max, the section must be enlarged.
   - Asv is the area of the two legs of a closed stirrup (mm²); sv its spacing (mm); b1 and d1 the centre-to-centre distances between the corner bars across the width and the depth (mm).
   - Mt is the equivalent moment from torsion; Mu the factored moment; D the overall depth, so D/b has no unit; Me1 is designed on the flexural tension face, Me2 on the compression face (see Combined bending, shear and torsion).
*/
