/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Flexural bond is needed wherever the bar force changes because the moment changes; anchorage bond is needed at a bar end, where the whole force must pass into the concrete.
   - Bond can fail in two ways.
   - A bar of diameter φ carries the force σs π φ²/4.
   - Ld is the development length (mm); φ is the bar diameter (mm); σs is the stress to be developed, 0.87 fy at the limit state of collapse (N/mm²); τbd is the design bond stress (N/mm²).
   - IS 456 gives τbd for plain bars in tension: 1.2 N/mm² for M20, 1.4 for M25, 1.5 for M30, 1.7 for M35 and 1.9 for M40 and above.
   - Ld depends on the bar diameter, the steel stress and the concrete grade only, not on the span or the load.
   - Checked at simple supports and points of inflection; it limits the bar diameter.
   - Near a support the shear is large, so the bar force climbs quickly from zero; M1/V is roughly the distance over which the moment reaches M1.
*/
