/* book Worked example: Q: Find the development length of a 16 mm Fe415 deformed bar in M25 concrete (a) in tension and (b) in compression. Also find the bond force such a bar must pass to the concrete.
   1. Design stress: σs = 0.87 × 415 = 361.05 N/mm².
   2. Bond stress in tension: τbd = 1.4 × 1.6 = 2.24 N/mm² (plain-bar value for M25 raised by 60 % for deformed bars).
   3. Tension: Ld = 16 × 361.05/(4 × 2.24) = 5776.8/8.96 = 644.7 mm, say 645 mm (40.3φ).
   4. Compression: τbd = 2.24 × 1.25 = 2.80 N/mm², so Ld = 5776.8/(4 × 2.80) = 515.8 mm, say 520 mm.
   5. Bar force: 361.05 × π × 16²/4 = 361.05 × 201.1 = 72,590 N = 72.6 kN. Check: 2.24 × π × 16 × 644.7 = 72,590 N, the same force passed by bond over Ld.
   Answer: Ld = 645 mm in tension and 520 mm in compression (515.8 mm computed); the bar force to be developed is 72.6 kN. */
