/* book Worked example 2: Q: At the simple support of the beam b = 300 mm, d = 550 mm (M25, Fe415), two 20 mm bottom bars continue into a 300 mm wide masonry support; the end cover is 30 mm. The shear at the support is V = 180 kN and the reaction compresses the bar ends. Check the anchorage.
   1. Development length: Ld = 20 × 361.05/(4 × 2.24) = 805.9 mm.
   2. Area at the support: Ast = 2 × 314.2 = 628.3 mm². Neutral axis: xu = 0.87 × 415 × 628.3/(0.36 × 25 × 300) = 84.0 mm.
   3. M1 = 0.87 × 415 × 628.3 × (550 − 0.416 × 84.0) = 226,850 × 515.1 = 116.84 × 10⁶ N·mm.
   4. 1.3 M1/V = 1.3 × 116.84 × 10⁶/(180 × 10³) = 843.9 mm.
   5. L0, straight bar beyond the support centre: 300/2 − 30 = 120 mm. So 1.3 M1/V + L0 = 843.9 + 120 = 963.9 mm ≥ Ld = 805.9 mm. The diameter is acceptable without a hook.
   6. Embedment into the support: Ld/3 = 268.6 mm; the bars have 300 − 30 = 270 mm from the face to their ends, just enough. Both bars, half of the four bars at midspan, reach the support, more than the one third required.
   Answer: Ld = 805.9 mm ≤ 1.3 M1/V + L0 = 963.9 mm, and the 270 mm embedment exceeds Ld/3 = 268.6 mm, so two straight 20 mm bars are adequately anchored. */
